data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies genomes and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- The Cas9 nuclease is delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), which lets the genome be cut at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable molecules that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- Natural CRISPR sequences occur in prokaryotes such as bacteria and archaea, where they help detect and destroy DNA from similar bacteriophages [2].\n- Headlines report applications, including treating diseases and eliminating HIV in cell culture, but the sources give no methods [4][5]."}

